The Breslow data and analysis suggests that smoking is a risk factor for coronary artery disease,
but it's effect varies with age. Since the analysis uses a Poisson regression model to compare coronary death incidence 
rates between smokers and non-smokers with person-years as units. The results show that smokers have higher incidence rates 
from ages 35-44 all the way until 65-74 with incidence rate ratios greater than 1. Although, the IRR decreases with age, from 5.737 
until 0.905 from the last age group of 75-84. The fact is that the relative effect of smoking weakens as it ages and it reverses when 
reaching the oldest age group. So, smoking appears to be an important risk factor, especially around the younger groups. But, these results 
describe associations in the observed data and do not establish causality themselves.
